Lojban gives you the choice to be precise or vague. That's what's great
about it. If you want to be precise about which place {ke'a} falls into,
then just say {ke'a}.

.i lo tcadu noi mi ke'a xabju cu barda mutce
The town which I live in is very big.

But if you want to be vague, just leave it out. It is left up to context
then.

.i lo cirla noi mi citka cu kukte mutce
The cheese which I am eating is very delicious.

This is in contrast to English, where you are forced to be precise in
places that you don't always care about (e.g. mandatory verb tense,
mandatory noun number).

If you look through the usage of Lojban over the past 15 or so years,
you'll see that what I've described is how NOI is used.

.i mi'e la tsani mu'o


